1. Scope of "the Software"
--------------------------

For the purpose of the license grant in the **MIT License** section below,
"the Software" means:

  (a) **Source code and scripts** in this repository (e.g. Python modules under
      `llm-safety-experiment/`, automation scripts, and configuration files
      intended for execution or integration);

  (b) **Documentation and markup** distributed alongside that code (e.g.
      Markdown reports, rubrics, reproducibility instructions, and manuscript
      drafts committed for transparency);

  (c) **Evaluation artifacts** shipped for replication, to the extent
      copyrightable in your jurisdiction and contributed by the copyright
      holder(s) above, including but not limited to: **fixed prompt banks**,
      **committed labeled result JSON**, **figure specification / metadata**
      produced by this project's scripts, and **small derived summaries**
      checked in to support reproducibility.

  (d) **Third-party model outputs** embedded in committed `results/*.json` are
      primarily **factual records** of API responses obtained during empirical
      evaluation. They are included for **research replication**. Their
      **downstream use** (copying, redistribution, or further publication) may
      implicate **provider terms of use**, **platform policies**, and
      **applicable law** independent of this license. This license does not
      grant any right to use third-party **models**, **trademarks**, or
      **services**—only the **repository materials** as defined here.

**Patents.** The MIT License below is a **copyright license** only. It does **not**
include an express **patent grant**; nothing herein shall be read as granting
patent rights unless you separately negotiate such rights with the copyright
holder(s).

**Third-party services.** Running new experiments may require **separate
agreements** with API providers (e.g. OpenAI, Anthropic, Google). This license
does **not** grant any right to use those services or their models; it governs
the **material in this repository**.

**Trademarks.** This license does not grant permission to use trade names,
trademarks, or service marks except as required for reasonable attribution.

2. Academic and professional attribution (community norm)
----------------------------------------------------------

The **MIT License** requires retaining the **copyright and permission notice**
when redistributing the Software. **Additionally**, academic and professional
derivatives (papers, theses, benchmarks, policy memos) are **expected** to cite
this project in a manner appropriate to the field—e.g. via the **preferred
name** *Multimodel-CEP-Benchmark*, the **repository URL**, and the **commit
hash** used for any reported numbers, as described in README.md and
CITATION.cff. This expectation is a **community norm** for reproducible
empirical work; it does not add contractual obligations beyond those in the
MIT License text below.

3. Responsible use (non-contractual summary)
-------------------------------------------

The repository is intended for **authorized safety research**, **scoped red
teaming**, and **education**. Prompt banks and model outputs may include
material that is disturbing or dual-use if misused. See **SECURITY.md** for
expectations around handling and redistribution. That document does not modify
the MIT License terms below; it describes project norms.

